From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 22/22] dndcp: Remove staging directories once their transfers
 are over

A host to guest file transfer is staged under
~/.cache/vmware/drag_and_drop, and nothing removed it afterwards. The
delete-on-reboot call after a paste unlinks the directory's own path,
which fails for a directory, so every transfer stayed on disk. The call
stays, as the X11 backends have it, and its comment now says that it
removes nothing on POSIX.

Nothing tells the guest when a file manager has finished copying out of
a staging directory, a paste can be repeated from the same clipboard
later, and an application can open a dropped file by its staging path
and save changes back to it. So a staging directory is removed only when
all of these hold:

- its name is one mkdtemp() made for it;
- it is not empty, since an empty one is reused for the next transfer,
  possibly a drag still in progress;
- the clipboard does not offer it, in the Wayland or the X11 backend,
  for a host file paste or for file contents, until another host or
  guest copy replaces that clip;
- no host to guest drag is still using it, until the host reports its
  files done;
- nothing in it has changed for two minutes;
- no process holds a file in it open or has it as its working directory.

The check runs when a new staging directory is made, when vmusr starts,
and once a minute from the main loop, in the Wayland and the X11
backend. A file manager copying out of the directory holds the file
open, so the two minutes count from the end of the copy. It reads /proc
once per run, and only when a directory is otherwise due. A tree with a
read-only directory from the host is made writable first, so that it
does not fail on every run.
